# ============================================================================
# THIS IS A TEMPLATE. Nothing reads it.
#
# It documents the two shapes below. Copy it into whichever SOURCE file you need,
# then activate that source:
#
#   cp .env.example .env.localstack   # then fill in shape A, then: npm run env:local
#   cp .env.example .env.remote       # then fill in shape B, then: npm run env:remote
#
#   npm run env:which                 # which is active right now
#
# `.env` is the ACTIVE COPY that the app and the tests actually read — do not edit it
# directly, edit the source and re-activate. (`npm run env:local` refuses to
# overwrite hand-edits to `.env` without `--force`, so a mistake here is recoverable.)
#
# `.dev.vars` is a symlink to `.env`, and that is load-bearing: the Worker reads
# `.dev.vars` while the test harness reads `.env`, so one file behind both means they
# can never disagree about a secret.
#
# !! NEVER CREATE `.env.local`, `.env.test` OR `.env.test.local`. !!
# `vitest.config.mts` calls loadEnv("test", …), and Vite reads those three at HIGHER
# precedence than `.env` — while the Worker keeps reading `.dev.vars` -> `.env`. Any
# of them silently gives the tests different values from the app. `.env.localstack`
# and `.env.remote` are safe precisely because loadEnv ignores them.
# ============================================================================
#
# ============================================================================
# There are TWO valid shapes for this file, and the difference is which database
# and auth server the app talks to.
#
#   A. THE LOCAL STACK (recommended for development, required for the tests).
#      `supabase start` runs Postgres on :54322 and GoTrue on :54321 in Docker,
#      and `supabase db reset` re-applies the migrations to a clean database in
#      seconds. Nothing you do can affect real data. The values are at the bottom
#      of this file under "LOCAL STACK", and they are not secret — they are the
#      Supabase CLI's fixed demo keys.
#
#      The test suite REQUIRES this: it drives `vite dev` over HTTP, so the worker
#      must be pointed at the database the tests assert against.
#
#   B. A REAL SUPABASE PROJECT. Use the values below, from the dashboard.
#      Necessary for testing real confirmation and reset email, and for anything
#      that has to behave exactly like production (see STACK.md trap 4 on the
#      pooler). Remember that this project becomes production once v2 ships.
#
# Copy to `.dev.vars` (or symlink it to `.env`). Whichever shape you use, fill in
# every uncommented key — `requireSecret` fails fast with an explanation rather
# than misbehaving later.
# ============================================================================

# --- Sessions -----------------------------------------------------------
# Signs the session cookie. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# In `vite dev` rwsdk falls back to a development key if this is unset, but
# production will refuse to start without it.
AUTH_SECRET_KEY=replace-me-with-openssl-rand-base64-32

# --- Supabase auth (teacher signup, sign-in, confirmation + reset emails) -
# Supabase Dashboard -> Project Settings -> API. See SUPABASE_SETUP.md.
# Students NEVER touch Supabase; they log in with a class code.
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=replace-me-anon-public-key

# !! SERVICE ROLE — bypasses every authorization rule in the Supabase project.
# Used only by `src/auth/provision.ts` (the seed/admin path). Never sent to a
# browser; never prefixed with VITE_.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=replace-me-service-role-key

# --- Supabase Postgres (the app database) -------------------------------
# MUST be the SUPAVISOR POOLER uri, from Dashboard -> Connect -> Transaction
# pooler. Port 6543.
#
# Do NOT use the direct connection (db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432): it is IPv6-only
# and Cloudflare Workers cannot open outbound IPv6 connections. The pooler is
# IPv4 on every tier, so this is a hard requirement, not a preference.
#
# Percent-encode any special characters in the password.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres

# --- Optional -----------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute public origin. Every confirmation and password-reset link is built
# from it (see getAppOrigin in src/lib/supabase.ts).
#
# LEAVE THIS UNSET LOCALLY. When absent, getAppOrigin falls back to the origin of
# the incoming request, which is what makes the emailed links point at
# http://localhost:5173 during development. Setting it here would send your dev
# signup links to production.
#
# PRODUCTION ONLY, and set as a Cloudflare secret rather than here:
#   npx wrangler secret put APP_URL      ->  https://classkudos.com
#
# It must match the deployed origin EXACTLY (scheme, host, no trailing slash) and
# be on Supabase's redirect allow-list, or Supabase silently substitutes its Site
# URL and the link loses its path.

# Sentry DSN. Leave unset to disable error reporting — both halves of it: the Worker
# disables the SDK (src/worker.tsx) and the document omits the client-side DSN
# entirely (src/app/Document.tsx), so nothing is ever half-configured.
#
# It does one more thing that is easy to miss: `setCommonHeaders` derives the CSP's
# `connect-src` from this value, so the browser may POST events to exactly this DSN's
# origin and nowhere else. Change the DSN and the policy follows automatically —
# which is the point, because a mismatch there is SILENT: events are captured, the
# browser refuses the request, and nothing arrives.
#
# The DSN is public by design; it authorises sending events and nothing else, which
# is why it is safe in page source.
# SENTRY_DSN=https://...ingest.us.sentry.io/...

# ============================================================================
# LOCAL STACK (shape A) — paste these over the five values above.
#
#   supabase start && supabase db reset && npm run seed     (= npm run test:db)
#
# The keys are the Supabase CLI's fixed local demo values, identical on every
# machine. They are not secrets and are safe to commit; `supabase start` prints
# them too.
#
#   SUPABASE_URL="http://127.0.0.1:54321"
#   SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJzdXBhYmFzZS1kZW1vIiwicm9sZSI6ImFub24iLCJleHAiOjE5ODM4MTI5OTZ9.CRXP1A7WOeoJeXxjNni43kdQwgnWNReilDMblYTn_I0"
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJzdXBhYmFzZS1kZW1vIiwicm9sZSI6InNlcnZpY2Vfcm9sZSIsImV4cCI6MTk4MzgxMjk5Nn0.EGIM96RAZx35lJzdJsyH-qQwv8Hdp7fsn3W0YpN81IU"
#   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres"
#
# A direct connection on 127.0.0.1 does NOT violate trap 4: the pooler is
# mandatory in production because the direct Supabase host is IPv6-only, and
# loopback is IPv4.
#
# Seeded teacher:  teacher@classkudos.local / changeme-please-8+
# Studio:          http://127.0.0.1:54323
# Mail catcher:    http://127.0.0.1:54324   (confirmation + reset email land here)
#
# --- Test-suite knobs (all optional) ---------------------------------------
#
# Where the harness reads and writes. Falls back to DATABASE_URL, which is
# normally right: the harness and the worker MUST see the same database.
#   TEST_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres"
#
# Pooler fidelity check. Production reaches Postgres only through the Supavisor
# TRANSACTION pooler, which a direct connection never exercises. `supabase start`
# runs one locally on :54329.
#
# Change DATABASE_URL, not TEST_DATABASE_URL — the thing under test is the
# WORKER's connection path. Moving only the harness's connection proves nothing.
#   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54329/postgres"
#
# Where the integration suite expects the dev server. Host AND port must match
# exactly: rwsdk refuses any action whose Origin differs from the server's own.
#   TEST_BASE_URL="http://localhost:5173"
#
# Make the suite FAIL rather than spawn a server, so you can run `npm run dev`
# yourself and watch its output — the only place a worker-side stack trace appears.
#   TEST_SERVER=external
# ============================================================================
